<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <title>{{ appName }}</title>
<?php
    /**
     * Stamp an asset URL with the file's modification time (?v=…), so a deploy
     * busts the browser cache without a build tool while unchanged assets stay
     * cached. __DIR__ is this shell's directory (the web root), where the assets
     * live too, so the stub is self-contained wherever it is copied.
     */
    $asset = static function (string $path): string {
        $mtime = @filemtime(__DIR__ . '/' . ltrim($path, '/')) ?: 0;
        return htmlspecialchars($path . '?v=' . $mtime, ENT_QUOTES);
    };
?>
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="<?php echo $asset('assets/css/app.css'); ?>">
</head>
<body>
    <!--
        Single-page-application shell for a services-oriented Pramnos app.

        A tiny PHP page — NOT an MVC view (it is not rendered through a theme or
        getView()). It emits the app-shell HTML and stamps each asset URL with the
        file's mtime for cache-busting, then boots the JS client, which talks to
        the JSON API (attribute-routed controllers in src/Controllers) over
        fetch(). There is no server-rendered view layer — the API is the contract,
        documented via `php pramnos api:docs`.

        Serve it from the web root alongside its assets; a thin front controller
        renders it for page requests (unmatched, non-API GETs), and it is directly
        reachable too. If you use a build tool that emits content-hashed filenames,
        prefer the static spa-index.html.stub instead — the hashing already busts
        the cache, so the PHP shell buys you nothing there.
    -->
    <div id="app">Loading…</div>
    <script src="<?php echo $asset('assets/js/app.js'); ?>" type="module"></script>
</body>
</html>
